 Let $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ be three vectors such that $\|\mathbf{a}\| = \|\mathbf{b}\| = \|\mathbf{c}\| = 2.$  Also, the angle between any two of these vectors is $\arccos \frac{5}{8}.$  Let's consider the matrix $A = [\mathbf{a}\ \mathbf{b}\ \mathbf{c}]$. Then the volume of the parallelepiped generated by $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ is given by $\Delta(A)$, which is the square root of the sum of the main diagonal of $A^TA$. So we have
\begin{align*}
A^TA &= \left[ \mathbf{a}\ \mathbf{b}\ \mathbf{c} \right]^T \left[ \mathbf{a}\ \mathbf{b}\ \mathbf{c} \right] \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{a}^T \\ \mathbf{b}^T \\ \mathbf{c}^T
\end{bmatrix}
= \mathbf{a}\mathbf{a}^T + \mathbf{b}\mathbf{b}^T + \mathbf{c}\mathbf{c}^T 
\end{align*}
By the identity $\|\mathbf{a}\| = 2$, we know that $\mathbf{a}\mathbf{a}^T$ has the value of a $2\times 2$ matrix whose main diagonal and elements are all $4$. Since the angle between any two vectors is $\arccos \frac{5}{8}$, then by the law of cosines, the value of each $\mathbf{x}\mathbf{y} = \|\mathbf{x}\| \|\mathbf{y}\| \cos (\mathbf{xy}) = 2 \times 2 \times \frac{5}{8} = 2.5$. Since the only non-zero elements of $A^TA$ are the main diagonal, we have that $\Delta(A)^2 = 12 + 3(2.5^2) = 30.75$. Hence, the volume is $\boxed{\sqrt{30.75}} <answer> </answer>

